VALUES

-- Q1: hard / L4 — Section 501(h) expenditure test vs substantial part test
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Hope Education Alliance, a 501(c)(3) with $800,000 in exempt-purpose expenditures, spends $130,000 on direct lobbying and $40,000 on grassroots lobbying in 2025. The organization has not made a Section 501(h) election. Evaluate whether the organization''s lobbying activities jeopardize its exempt status under both the substantial part test and the expenditure test framework.', '["Lobbying at over 21% of expenditures is likely substantial and risks loss of exempt status; the 501(h) election would have provided defined dollar safe harbors", "The lobbying is permissible under both frameworks because it is below 20% of total expenditures", "The 501(h) election is irrelevant because the substantial part test always applies regardless of any election", "The organization automatically loses its exempt status because any lobbying by a 501(c)(3) is prohibited"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), lobbying is permitted if it does not constitute a "substantial part" of activities. Without the Section 501(h) election, the vague substantial part test applies, and $170,000 of total lobbying on $800,000 expenditures (over 21%) would likely be considered substantial. The 501(h) election provides defined dollar limits as a safe harbor. Unlike lobbying, political campaign activity—not lobbying—is absolutely prohibited.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Dissolution clause requirement
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What must a 501(c)(3) organization''s governing documents include regarding the distribution of assets upon dissolution?', '["A provision allowing distribution to any organization chosen by the board", "A dissolution clause requiring assets to be distributed to another exempt organization or the government", "A requirement that assets be returned proportionally to original donors", "A clause directing all assets to the organization''s officers and directors"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the organizational test requires that governing documents include a dissolution clause providing that upon dissolution, remaining assets will be distributed to another Section 501(c)(3) organization or to the government for a public purpose. Distribution to donors, officers, or any non-exempt entity would violate this requirement and disqualify the organization from tax-exempt status.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Supporting organization classification (509(a)(3))
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Foundation for Regional Hospitals, a 501(c)(3) entity, is operated exclusively to fundraise for and distribute grants to three designated public charity hospitals. It has no independent charitable programs and receives all direction from the hospitals'' boards. How is this organization most likely classified under Section 509?', '["Private foundation subject to Sections 4940 through 4945", "Public charity under the one-third support test of Section 509(a)(1)", "Supporting organization under Section 509(a)(3) because it operates exclusively to support specified public charities", "Publicly supported organization under the gross receipts test of Section 509(a)(2)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(3), a supporting organization is classified as a public charity—not a private foundation—when it is organized and operated exclusively to support one or more specified public charities. Foundation for Regional Hospitals fits this classification because it exists solely to support designated public charity hospitals. This differs from the Section 509(a)(1) one-third support test, which measures broad public funding rather than the relationship to supported organizations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: easy / L2 — Form 990-N e-Postcard threshold
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Neighborhood Watch Coalition, a small 501(c)(3), has annual gross receipts of $38,000 and total assets of $45,000. Which annual information return must this organization file with the IRS?', '["Form 990 (full return)", "Form 990-EZ (short form)", "Form 990-PF (private foundation return)", "Form 990-N (e-Postcard)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 6033, organizations with gross receipts normally $50,000 or less may file Form 990-N (e-Postcard), which is a simple electronic notice containing the organization''s name, address, EIN, and confirmation of continued eligibility. With $38,000 in gross receipts, Neighborhood Watch Coalition qualifies for the e-Postcard. Unlike Form 990-EZ—which requires more detailed financial reporting—the e-Postcard is the simplest filing option for small organizations.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Exempt purposes under 501(c)(3)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which of the following is an exempt purpose under Section 501(c)(3)?', '["Prevention of cruelty to children or animals", "Generating investment returns for shareholders", "Distributing profits to organizational members", "Providing below-market loans to corporate insiders"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), exempt purposes include religious, charitable, scientific, testing for public safety, literary, educational, fostering national or international amateur sports competition, and prevention of cruelty to children or animals. Generating investment returns, distributing profits, and providing insider loans are not exempt purposes and would violate the private inurement prohibition.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Convenience exclusion from UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Lakewood University, a 501(c)(3) institution, operates an on-campus laundry facility available only to enrolled students living in dormitories. The laundry generates $75,000 in annual revenue. The university''s CFO questions whether this income is subject to UBIT. How should this income be classified?', '["Subject to UBIT because laundry services are unrelated to the educational mission", "Subject to UBIT because the revenue exceeds the $1,000 specific deduction", "Partially subject to UBIT based on the percentage used by non-students", "Excluded from UBIT under the convenience exclusion because the activity serves students of the educational institution"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 513(a)(2), income from a trade or business carried on primarily for the convenience of members, students, patients, or employees is excluded from UBIT even if it meets the three-part test. Because Lakewood University''s laundry facility serves only enrolled dormitory students, the convenience exclusion applies. The $1,000 specific deduction under Section 512(b)(12) is separate and does not determine whether income is subject to UBIT in the first place.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Controlled entity payments as UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Bright Future Foundation, a 501(c)(3), owns 100% of a for-profit subsidiary, TechServe Inc. TechServe pays Bright Future $200,000 annually in rent for office space. Fair market value rent for comparable space is $150,000. Under the controlled entity rules, how is this rent treated for UBIT purposes?', '["Entirely excluded from UBIT as passive rental income from real property", "The $50,000 excess over fair market value is included in UBIT", "The entire $200,000 is included in UBIT because of the controlled entity relationship", "Only the $150,000 fair market value portion is included in UBIT"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(13), rent, interest, or royalties received from a controlled subsidiary (80% or more ownership) are treated as unrelated business taxable income to the extent the payment exceeds fair market value. Since TechServe pays $200,000 versus the $150,000 FMV, the $50,000 excess is included in UBIT. The general passive income exclusion for real property rents does not fully apply to payments from controlled entities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — Personal property rent 10% incidental rule
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'River Valley Education Trust, a 501(c)(3), leases a building to a commercial tenant for $120,000 per year. The lease also includes office furniture and equipment valued at a rent of $15,000 per year, making total rent $135,000. The property is not debt-financed. Apply the personal property rent rules to determine the UBIT treatment of this lease income.', '["The entire $135,000 is excluded from UBIT as rental income from real property", "The entire $135,000 is included in UBIT because the lease includes personal property", "The $15,000 personal property rent is included in UBIT while the $120,000 real property rent is excluded because personal property rent exceeds 10% of total rent", "Only the $120,000 is excluded; the $15,000 is included regardless of the percentage"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(3), rents from real property are excluded from UBIT, but rents from personal property are not. When a lease includes both, personal property rent is excluded only if it is incidental—defined as less than 10% of total rent. Here, personal property rent ($15,000) is 11.1% of total rent ($135,000), exceeding the 10% threshold. Therefore the $15,000 personal property portion is included in UBIT while the real property rent remains excluded.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: easy / L1 — Filing deadline for Form 990
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the filing deadline for Form 990 for a calendar-year tax-exempt organization?', '["January 31", "March 15", "April 15", "May 15"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 6033, Form 990 is due on the 15th day of the 5th month after the end of the organization''s tax year. For a calendar-year organization (year ending December 31), this means May 15. Extensions of up to six months are available using Form 8868. Unlike corporate returns (due April 15) and partnership returns (due March 15), exempt organization returns have a later deadline.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Public charity vs private foundation classification analysis
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Global Research Initiative, a 501(c)(3), received the following support over the past four years: $1,200,000 from a single wealthy donor, $300,000 from government grants, $200,000 from small individual contributions (none exceeding $5,000), and $100,000 from investment income. Evaluate whether this organization qualifies as a public charity under the one-third support test of Section 509(a)(1) and explain the consequences if it fails.', '["The organization fails the one-third support test because public support ($500,000) represents only 27.8% of total support ($1,800,000), making it a private foundation subject to excise taxes under Sections 4940 through 4945", "The organization passes because the single donor''s $1,200,000 counts as public support", "The organization is automatically a public charity because it received government grants", "The organization qualifies as a public charity because total contributions exceed $1,000,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and Section 170(b)(1)(A)(vi), an organization qualifies as a public charity if it receives at least one-third of its support from the general public and government. Contributions from a single donor exceeding 2% of total support are limited. Here, public support (government grants plus broad public contributions) totals approximately $500,000 of $1,800,000 total support (27.8%), falling short of the one-third threshold. As a private foundation, Sections 4940–4945 excise taxes apply. Receiving government grants alone does not guarantee public charity status.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Research exclusion from UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Westfield University, a 501(c)(3), receives $400,000 from a federal agency to conduct medical research, and $250,000 from a pharmaceutical company for proprietary drug research where results are confidential. Which research income is excluded from UBIT?', '["The $400,000 government-funded research is excluded from UBIT, but the $250,000 proprietary research is not excluded because results are not publicly available", "Both amounts are excluded because all research by a university is automatically exempt", "Neither amount is excluded because research is always treated as a trade or business", "Only the $250,000 is excluded because private-sector research is always related to the university''s exempt purpose"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(7)–(9), research performed for the U.S. government or by a college or university is excluded from UBIT. However, research funded by private entities is excluded only if the results are freely available to the general public. Since the pharmaceutical company''s research results are proprietary and confidential, the $250,000 does not qualify for the research exclusion. The $400,000 government-funded research is excluded regardless of whether results are published.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Form 990-PF for all private foundations
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Henderson Family Foundation, a private foundation under Section 509, has gross receipts of only $25,000 and total assets of $400,000. The foundation''s accountant suggests filing Form 990-N because of the low gross receipts. Is this advice correct?', '["Yes, private foundations with gross receipts under $50,000 may file Form 990-N", "Yes, but only if the foundation also has assets under $500,000", "No, the foundation should file Form 990-EZ because assets exceed $200,000", "No, all private foundations must file Form 990-PF regardless of their size"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 6033(a), all private foundations must file Form 990-PF regardless of their gross receipts or total assets. The Form 990-PF includes detailed reporting on investments, grants, and excise tax computations under Sections 4940–4945. Unlike public charities, which may file the simplified Form 990-N or 990-EZ based on size thresholds, private foundations have no simplified filing option. The accountant''s advice is incorrect because the private foundation designation overrides gross receipts-based alternatives.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: easy / L1 — Private inurement prohibition
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What does the private inurement prohibition under Section 501(c)(3) prevent?', '["All compensation payments to employees of the organization", "Net earnings of the organization from benefiting any private shareholder or individual who is an insider", "The organization from earning any revenue from business activities", "Tax-exempt organizations from investing in securities"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the private inurement prohibition provides that no part of the net earnings of the organization may inure to the benefit of any private shareholder or individual. This applies to insiders such as founders, directors, and officers. It does not prohibit reasonable compensation, earning business revenue, or investing in securities—it specifically targets the improper transfer of organizational earnings to those with a personal stake in the entity.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Form 8868 extension
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the purpose of Form 8868 for a tax-exempt organization?', '["To apply for initial tax-exempt status", "To report unrelated business taxable income", "To request an automatic extension of time to file Form 990", "To elect the Section 501(h) expenditure test for lobbying"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 6081, Form 8868 is used by tax-exempt organizations to request an automatic extension of up to six months to file their annual information return (Form 990, 990-EZ, 990-PF, or 990-T). Unlike Form 1023, which is used for initial exemption applications, Form 8868 addresses only filing deadlines. Form 990-T reports unrelated business income, and Form 5768 is used to make the Section 501(h) lobbying expenditure election.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — Section 4941 manager penalty calculation
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Cedar Grove Foundation, a private foundation, purchases a commercial property from its founder for $300,000 at fair market value. The foundation''s executive director, who is also a board member, knowingly approves the transaction believing FMV makes it permissible. Calculate the initial excise taxes on both the founder and the executive director under Section 4941.', '["$15,000 on the founder and $30,000 on the executive director", "$30,000 on the founder and $15,000 on the executive director", "No tax because the transaction was at fair market value", "$60,000 on the founder and no tax on the executive director"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 4941, any sale or exchange between a private foundation and a disqualified person (here, the founder) constitutes self-dealing regardless of price. The initial excise tax is 10% of the amount involved on the self-dealer ($300,000 x 10% = $30,000) and 5% on any foundation manager who knowingly participates ($300,000 x 5% = $15,000). Fair market value is irrelevant—self-dealing is based on the nature of the transaction, not the terms.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — One-third support test computation
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Sunrise Arts Collective, a 501(c)(3), reports four-year cumulative support of: $180,000 in small individual donations (none over $5,000), $120,000 in government grants, $90,000 from a single corporate sponsor, and $60,000 in investment income. Apply the Section 509(a)(1) one-third support test to determine if the organization qualifies as a public charity.', '["It qualifies because total contributions exceed $300,000", "It fails because investment income is not counted as public support", "It fails because the corporate sponsor''s contribution must be entirely excluded", "It qualifies because public support of $300,000 represents two-thirds of total support ($450,000), exceeding the one-third threshold"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1), an organization qualifies as a public charity if at least one-third of its total support comes from public sources (government grants and broad public contributions). Total support is $450,000. Public support includes small individual donations ($180,000), government grants ($120,000), and the corporate sponsor contribution (counted in full here as it does not exceed 2% of total support). Public support totals $390,000, which is 86.7% of $450,000—well above one-third. Investment income ($60,000) counts as total support but not as public support.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — NOL deduction for UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Heritage Museum, a 501(c)(3), had unrelated business taxable income of ($45,000) in 2024 (a net operating loss). In 2025, the museum has $70,000 of gross unrelated business income, $15,000 of directly connected deductions, and the $1,000 specific deduction. Apply the UBIT NOL carryforward to calculate 2025 unrelated business taxable income.', '["$9,000, calculated as $70,000 gross UBI minus $15,000 deductions minus $1,000 specific deduction minus $45,000 NOL carryforward", "$54,000, because the NOL cannot offset unrelated business income", "$55,000, because the NOL deduction is not permitted for tax-exempt organizations", "$24,000, because only 50% of the NOL may be carried forward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(6), a net operating loss deduction is allowed for UBIT purposes, computed using only unrelated business income items. Heritage Museum''s 2025 calculation: $70,000 gross UBI minus $15,000 deductions = $55,000, minus the $1,000 specific deduction under Section 512(b)(12) = $54,000, minus the $45,000 NOL carryforward from 2024 = $9,000 UBIT. The NOL deduction for UBIT is computed separately from any exempt-activity income or losses.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Section 4945 taxable expenditures (grants to individuals)
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Pinnacle Arts Foundation, a private foundation, awards $25,000 scholarships to five individual artists based on a competitive selection process. The foundation did not obtain advance IRS approval for the grant procedures. Determine whether these grants are taxable expenditures under Section 4945 and calculate any applicable excise tax.', '["The grants are not taxable expenditures because they are for educational purposes", "The grants are taxable expenditures totaling $125,000, subject to a 20% initial excise tax of $25,000 on the foundation", "The grants are permissible because the amount per individual is under $50,000", "No excise tax applies because the selection process was competitive"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 4945(d)(3), grants by a private foundation to individuals for travel, study, or similar purposes are taxable expenditures unless the foundation obtains advance IRS approval of its grant-making procedures. Without such approval, all five grants totaling $125,000 are taxable expenditures. The initial excise tax is 20% of the expenditure amount ($125,000 x 20% = $25,000). A competitive selection process alone does not satisfy the advance approval requirement.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Bingo game exclusion from UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Which activity is specifically excluded from UBIT by statutory provision?', '["Operating a year-round commercial parking lot", "Selling advertising space in the organization''s magazine", "Conducting legal bingo games where commercial bingo is not ordinarily carried out in the jurisdiction", "Renting personal property to unrelated third parties"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 513(f), bingo games are specifically excluded from UBIT when they are legal under state and local law and commercial bingo is not regularly carried on in the same jurisdiction. Operating a parking lot, selling advertising, and renting personal property can all be subject to UBIT if they meet the three-part test. The bingo exclusion is one of several specific statutory carve-outs from unrelated business income.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: hard / L3 — Donated goods + volunteer labor exclusion interaction
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Goodwill Community Services, a 501(c)(3), operates two retail stores. Store A sells donated clothing and is staffed by 90% unpaid volunteers and 10% paid employees. Store B sells a mix of 60% donated goods and 40% purchased inventory, staffed entirely by paid employees. Store A generates $200,000 and Store B generates $300,000 in annual revenue. Analyze the UBIT exclusions applicable to each store.', '["Both stores are fully excluded from UBIT because the organization is a 501(c)(3) charity", "Store A is excluded only under the volunteer labor exclusion; Store B is fully subject to UBIT", "Store A qualifies for exclusion under both the volunteer labor and donated goods exclusions; Store B does not qualify for either exclusion because substantially all goods are not donated and it uses paid labor", "Both stores are subject to UBIT because retail sales always constitute unrelated business activity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 513(a)(1)–(2), UBIT exclusions apply when substantially all work is performed by unpaid volunteers or substantially all merchandise is donated. Store A qualifies under both exclusions—substantially all labor is volunteer and goods are donated. Store B fails both: it uses 100% paid staff (no volunteer exclusion) and only 60% donated goods does not meet "substantially all" for the donated goods exclusion. Each exclusion is evaluated independently, and either one alone is sufficient to exclude income from UBIT.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q21: hard / L4 — Intermediate sanctions 200% additional tax
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Riverside Health Alliance, a 501(c)(3), pays its CFO a $1,200,000 salary when the comparability data shows $400,000 is reasonable for the position. The board did not use an independent compensation study. After the IRS assessment of the initial 25% excise tax, the CFO refuses to return the $800,000 excess benefit during the correction period. Evaluate the total excise tax liability on the CFO and any tax on the participating board members under Section 4958.', '["$200,000 initial tax on the CFO only, with no additional consequences", "$200,000 initial tax on the CFO; the board is solely liable for any additional tax", "Only the board members are liable for excise taxes because they approved the compensation", "$200,000 initial tax plus $1,600,000 additional tax on the CFO for failure to correct; board members who knowingly participated owe 10% of the excess benefit up to $20,000 each"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under IRC Section 4958, the initial excise tax on the disqualified person (CFO) is 25% of the $800,000 excess benefit = $200,000. If the excess benefit is not corrected within the taxable period, an additional tax of 200% applies ($800,000 x 200% = $1,600,000). Total CFO liability: $200,000 + $1,600,000 = $1,800,000. Board members who knowingly participated owe 10% of the excess benefit ($80,000), capped at $20,000 per transaction per manager. The absence of a rebuttable presumption of reasonableness shifts the burden of proof to the organization.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q22: medium / L3 — Section 4943 combined holdings calculation
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Mitchell Family Foundation, a private foundation, owns 12% of the voting stock of Apex Manufacturing Corp. The Mitchell family members (disqualified persons) own an additional 15% of Apex''s voting stock. Under Section 4943, determine whether the combined holdings create excess business holdings and calculate any excise tax if Apex stock is valued at $5,000,000 for the foundation''s share.', '["The combined 27% exceeds the 20% permitted holding by 7%, resulting in a 10% initial excise tax of $500,000 on the excess", "No excess exists because the foundation''s 12% alone is under 20%", "The entire 27% is subject to the 10% excise tax", "Only the family''s 15% is subject to the excise tax"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 4943, combined holdings of a private foundation and its disqualified persons may not exceed 20% of voting stock in a business enterprise. The Mitchell Family Foundation (12%) plus disqualified persons (15%) = 27%, exceeding the 20% limit by 7%. The 7% excess, valued proportionally at $5,000,000 x (7/12) = approximately $2,916,667 of the foundation''s holdings, is subject to a 10% initial excise tax. The foundation must look at the combined ownership—not just its own holdings in isolation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Form 990 penalty calculation
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Coastal Conservation League, a 501(c)(3) with gross receipts of $450,000, files its Form 990 185 days late without reasonable cause. Calculate the late-filing penalty under Section 6652(c).', '["$20 per day for 185 days = $3,700", "$100 per day for 185 days = $18,500", "$20 per day, capped at $10,500 (the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of $450,000)", "$20 per day, capped at $22,500 (5% of $450,000)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 6652(c), the penalty for late filing of Form 990 for organizations with gross receipts under $1,186,000 is $20 per day the return is late, capped at the lesser of $10,500 or 5% of gross receipts. Coastal Conservation League: $20 x 185 days = $3,700. The cap is the lesser of $10,500 or 5% x $450,000 ($22,500) = $10,500. Since $3,700 is below the $10,500 cap, the full $3,700 penalty applies. Larger organizations (gross receipts over $1,186,000) face $100 per day up to $53,000.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Churches/schools/hospitals as automatic public charities
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Grace Community Church, a 501(c)(3) religious organization, receives 95% of its funding from a single wealthy family that founded the church. The church files for recognition of its tax-exempt status. How is the church classified for purposes of the public charity vs. private foundation distinction?', '["Private foundation, because a single family provides nearly all funding", "Public charity under the one-third support test because contributions are from the public", "Public charity, because churches are automatically classified as public charities regardless of funding sources under Section 509(a)(1)", "Supporting organization under Section 509(a)(3)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 509(a)(1) and Section 170(b)(1)(A)(i), churches are automatically classified as public charities regardless of their funding sources. This classification also applies to schools and hospitals. The one-third support test does not need to be met because the automatic public charity designation overrides the funding analysis. Therefore, even though a single family provides 95% of funding, Grace Community Church is still a public charity.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Private benefit vs private inurement distinction
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Horizon Learning Center, a 501(c)(3), contracts with an unrelated for-profit company to provide exclusive tutoring services at a rate 40% above market price. The company''s owner has no relationship with Horizon''s board or officers. Separately, Horizon''s treasurer receives a $50,000 bonus not authorized by the board. Evaluate which doctrine—private benefit or private inurement—applies to each transaction and the legal significance of the distinction.', '["Both transactions constitute private inurement because they involve payments above reasonable levels", "The tutoring contract is private benefit (outsider); the bonus is private inurement (insider); only inurement can trigger automatic revocation", "Only the treasurer''s bonus is problematic; the tutoring contract is permissible if it serves the educational mission", "Both transactions are permissible if the organization can demonstrate charitable intent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), private inurement specifically prohibits organizational earnings from flowing to insiders (officers, directors, founders). The treasurer''s unauthorized bonus is private inurement. The private benefit doctrine is broader—it prohibits excessive benefits to any person, insider or not. The above-market tutoring contract benefits an unrelated outsider, so it is private benefit, not private inurement. The distinction matters because private inurement can result in automatic loss of exempt status, whereas private benefit is evaluated on a facts-and-circumstances basis using a balancing test.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: hard / L4 — Controlled entity rent exceeding FMV as UBIT
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Pacific Education Foundation, a 501(c)(3), owns 85% of Coastal Leasing LLC and 100% of Harbor Services Inc. Coastal Leasing pays Pacific $180,000 annual rent (FMV is $160,000). Harbor Services pays Pacific $95,000 in royalties (FMV is $95,000). Pacific also receives $50,000 in dividends from publicly traded stocks. Evaluate the UBIT treatment of each income stream under the controlled entity and passive income rules.', '["All three income streams are excluded from UBIT as passive income", "Only the $50,000 in dividends is included in UBIT; rents and royalties are always excluded", "The $20,000 excess rent from Coastal Leasing is included in UBIT; the Harbor Services royalties and stock dividends are excluded because they do not exceed FMV", "All income from both controlled entities is included in UBIT regardless of FMV"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 512(b)(13), payments of rent, interest, annuities, and royalties from a controlled entity (80%+ ownership) are included in UBIT to the extent they exceed fair market value. Coastal Leasing (85% owned) pays $20,000 above FMV rent—this excess is UBIT. Harbor Services (100% owned) pays royalties at exactly FMV—no excess, so no UBIT. Dividends from publicly traded stocks are passive income excluded under Section 512(b)(1) and are not affected by controlled entity rules. The controlled entity provision targets only above-FMV payments.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Section 4940 excise tax rate
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the excise tax rate imposed on a private foundation''s net investment income under Section 4940?', '["0.5%", "1.39%", "2.0%", "5.0%"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 4940, private foundations are subject to a 1.39% excise tax on their net investment income, which includes interest, dividends, rents, royalties, and capital gains. This tax applies annually regardless of the foundation''s charitable activities. The rate was reduced from 2% to 1.39% by the Taxpayer Certainty and Disaster Tax Relief Act of 2019, eliminating the prior two-tier rate structure.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: medium / L2 — Form 990-EZ thresholds
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Harmony Youth League, a 501(c)(3) public charity, has gross receipts of $175,000 and total assets of $480,000 for the current tax year. Which annual information return must the organization file?', '["Form 990-N (e-Postcard) because gross receipts are under $200,000", "Form 990-EZ because gross receipts are under $200,000 and total assets are under $500,000", "Form 990 (full return) because total assets exceed $200,000", "Form 990-PF because total assets exceed $400,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under IRC Section 6033, a public charity may file Form 990-EZ if gross receipts are less than $200,000 AND total assets are less than $500,000. Harmony Youth League meets both criteria ($175,000 receipts and $480,000 assets). Unlike Form 990-N, which is only for organizations with gross receipts of $50,000 or less, Form 990-EZ requires more detailed financial reporting. Form 990-PF is exclusively for private foundations, not public charities.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Operational test requirements
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'Green Valley Education Fund, a 501(c)(3), spends 70% of its budget on educational programs, 15% on administrative costs, and 15% operating a commercial printing business whose profits fund the educational programs. The IRS examines whether the organization meets the operational test. What is the most likely outcome?', '["The organization passes the operational test because it uses all profits for exempt purposes", "The organization fails because administrative costs exceed 10%", "The organization passes because it is operated primarily for exempt purposes, with the commercial activity constituting an insubstantial part of total operations", "The organization fails because any commercial activity automatically disqualifies it from exempt status"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under IRC Section 501(c)(3), the operational test requires that an organization be operated primarily for exempt purposes. An organization may engage in some non-exempt activities (such as a commercial printing business) as long as they are not more than an insubstantial part of its operations. With 70% on educational programs and only 15% on commercial activity, Green Valley is primarily operated for exempt purposes. The key is that profits from commercial activity being used for exempt purposes does not alone make the activity related—but the activity''s insubstantial scope preserves compliance.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Section 501(h) election purpose
(3, 'Tax-Exempt Organizations', 'What is the purpose of the Section 501(h) election for a 501(c)(3) organization?', '["To provide specific dollar thresholds as a safe harbor for permissible lobbying expenditures", "To allow the organization to participate in political campaign activities", "To exempt the organization from filing Form 990", "To waive the private foundation excise taxes under Sections 4940 through 4945"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under IRC Section 501(h), eligible 501(c)(3) organizations may elect the expenditure test, which replaces the vague "substantial part" test for lobbying with specific dollar thresholds based on exempt-purpose expenditures. This election provides a safe harbor—organizations that stay within the limits know they will not lose their exempt status for lobbying. The election does not affect the absolute prohibition on political campaign activity, filing requirements, or private foundation excise taxes.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
